      sayEn: 'The writer passes the sluggard’s field and sees it overgrown with thorns, its wall broken down (24:30–31); he looks and receives instruction (24:32): a little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands (24:33), and poverty comes like a robber (24:34).',
      meanZh: '这是一则生动的观察寓言。荒废从来不是一夜发生的，而是无数个“再睡一会儿”累积的结果。这段话的智慧不在于责骂懒惰，而在于教我们从观察中学习：留心那些被忽视、正在长草的“田地”——可能是关系、健康、信仰或工作——趁石墙还没完全坍塌，就动手修补。',
      meanEn: 'This is a vivid observational parable. Ruin never happens overnight; it’s the accumulation of countless “just a little more sleep.” The wisdom here isn’t about scolding laziness but about learning by observation: notice the neglected “fields” growing weeds — a relationship, health, faith, work — and mend the wall before it fully collapses.',
      forMeZh: '我生命里有没有一块正在“长草”的田地，被我一再拖延、忽视？今天，我可以为它做的第一件小小的修补是什么？',
      forMeEn: 'Is there a “field” in my life quietly growing weeds through repeated delay and neglect? What is one small repair I can make to it today?',
