  realLife: [
    { tag: 'ENVY', tagZh: '嫉妒', verseRef: 'Proverbs 24:1',
      titleZh: '当作恶的人看起来赢了', titleEn: 'When the people cutting corners seem to win',
      bodyZh: '箴言 24:1–2、19–20 反复提醒：不要嫉妒恶人，也不要起意与他们相处。这不是叫我们瞧不起谁，而是在处理一种很真实的心结——当那些不择手段的人看起来更成功、更风光，我们心里那句“做好人有什么用”就悄悄冒出来。经文的回答是：看清结局。他们的“灯”终必熄灭，他们没有真正的将来。羡慕一样正在熄灭的东西，是不值得的。把眼光放长，就不再那么容易被一时的风光扰动。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 24:1–2, 19–20 repeatedly warns: don’t envy evil men or desire their company. It isn’t a call to look down on anyone; it addresses a very real knot in the heart — when the ruthless seem more successful and more admired, that quiet “what’s the point of being good?” creeps in. The answer of the text is: see the ending. Their “lamp” will be snuffed out; they have no lasting future. It isn’t worth envying something already going dark. Take the long view, and the momentary flash loses its power to unsettle you.' },
    { tag: 'RESILIENCE', tagZh: '坚韧', verseRef: 'Proverbs 24:16',
      titleZh: '跌倒了七次，还要起来', titleEn: 'Down seven times, up again',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 24:16 is one of the most quietly hopeful lines in the book: “a righteous man falls seven times and rises up again.” Notice what it does not say — it doesn’t say the righteous never fall. Righteousness isn’t a flawless record; it’s a direction, marked by getting back up. If you’ve failed — morally, in a relationship, at work — the enemy’s voice says, “See, this is just who you are.” This verse says something else: the fall is not the verdict. By God’s grace, getting up is. What matters is not that you fell but which way you face now.',
      bodyZh: '箴言 24:16 是全书中最安静、也最有盼望的一句：“义人虽七次跌倒，仍必兴起。”请留意它没有说什么——它没有说义人从不跌倒。义不是一份毫无瑕疵的记录，而是一个方向，以“重新站起来”为标志。如果你失败了——在道德上、在关系里、在工作中——那控告的声音会说：“看吧，你这个人就是这样。”而这节经文说的是另一回事：跌倒不是定论。靠着神的恩典，起来才是。要紧的不是你曾跌倒，而是你此刻面向哪个方向。' },
    { tag: 'RESPONSIBILITY', tagZh: '责任', verseRef: 'Proverbs 24:11',
      titleZh: '“这不关我的事”', titleEn: '“It’s not my problem”',
      bodyZh: '箴言 24:11–12 是一段让人无法轻松略过的话：“人被拉到死地，你要解救……你若说：这事我未曾知道，那衡量人心的岂不明白吗？”我们常用“我不知道”“不确定”“不关我事”来给自己的旁观找台阶。但经文指出，神看得见我们心里真实的选择。这不是要我们背起全世界的重担，而是提醒：当危难就在眼前、我们又有能力伸手时，选择转身离开，不是无辜的中立。智慧愿意被别人的处境打扰。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 24:11–12 is a passage hard to skip past lightly: “Rescue those who are being led away to death… If you say, ‘Behold, we didn’t know this,’ doesn’t he who weighs the hearts consider it?” We often use “I didn’t know,” “I’m not sure,” or “not my business” to excuse standing by. But the text says God sees the real choice in our hearts. This isn’t a call to carry the whole world’s weight; it’s a reminder that when peril is right in front of us and we have the power to reach out, turning away is not innocent neutrality. Wisdom is willing to be inconvenienced by another person’s plight.' },
    { tag: 'DILIGENCE', tagZh: '勤奋', verseRef: 'Proverbs 24:30',
      titleZh: '荒田是慢慢长出来的', titleEn: 'A ruined field grows slowly',
      bodyZh: '箴言 24:30–34 讲了一个观察：作者路过懒惰人的田地，看见荆棘蔓草长满、石墙坍塌，于是领受了训诲——荒废不是一夜之间的事，而是无数个“再睡一会儿、再拖一下”累积的结果。我们的生命里也常有这样的“田地”：一段疏于经营的关系、一个被忽略的健康信号、一份久未打理的信仰。它们不会突然崩塌，而是慢慢长草。智慧就是趁石墙还没塌，今天就去补上一小块。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 24:30–34 records an observation: the writer passes a sluggard’s field, sees it overgrown with thorns and its wall broken down, and draws a lesson — ruin isn’t an overnight event but the accumulation of countless “just a little more sleep, just a little more delay.” Our lives have such “fields” too: a relationship left untended, a health warning ignored, a faith long neglected. They don’t collapse suddenly; they slowly grow weeds. Wisdom mends one small section of the wall today, before it fully caves in.' },
  ],

